I'm Laura Kiesel and I have chronic pain  and I work as a freelance writer and  editor the onset of chronic pain was  really gradual for me it didn't happen  suddenly with an accident or anything in  particular I was born with a really  severe case of clubfoot I had to have as  a child several surgeries and wear a  cast until I was three and that left me  with some structural discrepancies and  issues in my body like I have my leg is  shorter on my left side than my right  and the the muscles in it are  underdeveloped and the foot has no arch  and is sort of pronated so over time as  I got older I had more and more stress  on my hips and my low spine so I always  had some pain as a child and as a  teenager but it hasn't been until the  past several years that it's become just  daily it was usually more sporadic or  intermittent growing up I also have  unrelated to that endometriosis which is  a disease a reproductive disease related  to like menstruation so starting in high  school I started getting very painful  periods and it wasn't until I was 24  that I was able to finally get surgery  to have a lot of it removed and have it  diagnosed but I had so I had pain during  those days and I had some other pain but  it wasn't daily until the past few years  where I've started to have a lot of  degenerative changes in my hips and  spine they think partially from that  discrepancy from my legs and just the  wear and tear as you grow older of  walking with those kind of structural  abnormalities kind of wearing on my body  so when when pain becomes a daily  reality it can be a very like hollowing  almost a humanizing experience after a  while because it's just you wake up and  you're still in pain and you go to bed  and you're still in pain and  you don't sleep well because you because  you're in pain so it can it can be very  distracting I think it's very hard until  you've had that be your daily reality to  realize how much it can affect your  concentration affect your mobility  affect your ability to do things that  you took for granted so so many things  that used to come very easily to me now  take a lot of effort and even sometimes  just bandwidth like the mental or  emotional bandwidth to deal with  something when I have a really bad pain  day is much lower on some days than  others some days I just can't handle a  workload at all and I just need to be in  bed or just take it easy and other days  I'll have a good day but even if I'm  very productive usually it's like then I  run on a deficit the following days for  having a high energy day it's never  nothing is ever free anymore it's like  you're running constantly on a certain  amount of borrowed time and bandwidth  and physical energy or tolerance to pain  I've only been offered not even really  what's considered pain medication  primarily but things like anticonvulsant  like gabapentin and lyrica which are  primarily for epilepsy but now are being  used for people with fibromyalgia and  nerve pain so I've tried those I didn't  have they didn't really help much they  took the edge off a little but not much  and then the side-effects kind of  outweighed any benefit so I stopped  taking them after a few months  other than NS aids or like ibuprofen and  and Tylenol  like I don't really have much else  that's been offered to me the only time  opioids have really been offered to me I  know now they're cutting back so it  might be the timing with which my  chronic pain has really blown up has  been really acute situations where  they've only given me a few days worth  if I've like when my disk first  herniated like I got a few days worth of  codeine the only thing I take  semi-regularly  is I take a flexural which is a muscle  relaxant a few nights a week when I  really need to try to sleep through the  pain most people I know in my chronic  pain group and friends who have had  similar issues are on opioids or at  least wore for time an extended period  of time but because I have a family  history of addiction  I really prevalent family history of  addiction to opioid drugs I did not want  to take that so I've never really  inquired and even when I have four very  acute situations like when I've in like  when my discs first got injured I made  sure to ask for like the lowest type in  the lowest dose because I just don't  want to even risk going down any kind of  pathway to addiction similar to like my  family members my mother was a heroin  addict she did not start with opioids  she she started straight from  experimenting with different drugs like  cocaine and then started with heroin and  she became a heroin addict my brother so  my mother was a heroin addict for many  years and then she got on a methadone  program and she wasn't on heroin anymore  but when she became a cancer patient and  she had terminal cancer for many years  it was terminal but it was it it was  five years before she died she was given  an opioid prescription I don't think  they did any evaluation of her history  or maybe they also figured just because  she is terminal to overlook her history  but my brother was in the household with  her she started giving him her pills so  he became addicted to dilaudid and when  she died he actually suffered very badly  from withdrawal because he suddenly  didn't have access to her prescriptions  anymore he took to the streets and he  got arrested and it he finally had to  check himself in for a couple of days  and then he got on suboxone and and got  off opioid
 

